Logic :

solved this problem using backtracking, similar to the normal permutation problem,
but with an additional step to handle duplicate values.

First, sort the input array so that duplicate elements become adjacent. This makes it easy to identify duplicates during recursion.
maintain a temporary list to build the current permutation and a boolean visited array to track which elements have already been used.
At each recursive call,  iterate through all the elements. If an element is already used,  skip it.
To avoid generating duplicate permutations, before choosing an element , check whether it is the same as its previous element. 
If it is a duplicate and the previous duplicate has not been used in the current recursive path,  skip the current element.
This ensures that it always pick the first occurrence of a duplicate before considering the next one, preventing identical permutation branches from being explored.
If the element is valid to use,  add it to the current permutation, mark it as visited, and recursively build the remaining permutation.
When the current permutation reaches the same size as the input array,  copy it into the final answer.
After returning from recursion,  backtrack it by removing the last element and marking it as unused. 
This restores the previous state so that it can explore the remaining choices.
By combining backtracking with the duplicate-skipping condition, the algorithm generates every unique permutation exactly once."


Code :


class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permuteUnique(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans=new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> list=new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[] f=new boolean[nums.length];
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
        return ans;
    }
    void permutation(int[] nums,boolean[] f,List<Integer> list,List<List<Integer>> ans){
        if(list.size()==nums.length){
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
            return;
        }
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(i>0 && nums[i]==nums[i-1] && !f[i-1]){
                continue;
            }
            if(!f[i]){
                list.add(nums[i]);
                f[i]=true;
                permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
                f[i]=false;
                list.remove(list.size()-1);
            }
        }
    }
}
